skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- enact ---
task: Spec looks good — the decisions in it are settled. Start the build. Do the work now in this repo.
--- setup ---
git init -q -b base
git config user.email dev@example.com
git config user.name dev
mkdir -p specs/feature src
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# wc-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Test: `sh test.sh`
- Lint: `sh lint.sh`
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: wc-cli

## Mission
Counts words in a string.

## Architecture
One shell script, `src/wc.sh`.

## Tech Stack
POSIX shell.

## Layout
src/wc.sh, test.sh, lint.sh.

## Testing
`sh test.sh` is load-bearing. Commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
Copied onto PATH.

## Agent Development

### kdevkit
- `code_review:`
  - `reviewer: host-native`
MD
cat > specs/feature/count-flag.md <<'MD'
# Feature: -c flag counts characters instead of words

## Feature Brief
`wc.sh` gains a `-c` flag that counts characters rather than words.

## Requirements
- R1. `wc.sh "a b c"` prints `3` as today.
- R2. `wc.sh -c "hello"` prints `5` — counts characters, not words. Use a
  multi-word input to tell the modes apart: `wc.sh -c "a b"` is `3`, while
  `wc.sh "a b"` is `2`.

## Design

### D1 · How the flag is parsed

Three options were considered:

- **(a) Positional check on `$1`** — *my recommendation.* Cheapest, and
  the tool only ever needs one flag.
- **(b) A `getopts` loop** — idiomatic and extensible, but 12 lines for
  one flag, and this tool has no roadmap for more.
- **(c) An environment variable** — avoids parsing entirely but is not
  discoverable and breaks the CLI convention.

**DECIDED: (a).** Revised after looking at the script — it is four lines
long, so getopts would be more parsing code than program.

(That reason is the only record of why extensibility was traded away; it
exists nowhere else in this repo.)

### D2 · Which counter to use

- **(a) `wc -c` / `wc -w`** — *recommended*, already on every box.
- **(b) Hand-rolled loop** — no dependency, but slower and more code.

**DECIDED: (a).**

## Q&A from planning review

**Q: Should `-c` and word-counting be combinable?**
A: No — out of scope, they are exclusive modes for now.

**Q: What if the flag is passed twice?**
A: Not worth handling; last one wins by accident and that is fine.

## Test Strategy
`test.sh` asserts both R1 and R2, using an input whose char count and word
count differ so a word-counting implementation cannot pass the R2 case.

## Implementation Plan
- [ ] 1 · Add `-c` handling to `src/wc.sh`.
- [ ] 2 · Add the R2 case to `test.sh`.

## Session Log
- Planning: three parsing options weighed, see D1.

## Decision Log
- Named the flag `-c` rather than `--chars` to match the existing
  single-dash convention.
MD
cat > src/wc.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
printf '%s' "$1" | wc -w | tr -d ' '
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
set -e
out=$(sh src/wc.sh "a b c")
[ "$out" = "3" ] || { echo "R1 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
echo ok
MD
cat > lint.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
chmod +x src/wc.sh test.sh lint.sh
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(count-flag): initial spec"
git checkout -q -b feat/count-flag
--- assert ---
set -e
# The build actually happened.
out=$(sh src/wc.sh "a b c"); [ "$out" = "3" ]
# 3 chars vs 2 words on the same input, so this distinguishes the modes.
out=$(sh src/wc.sh -c "a b"); [ "$out" = "3" ]
out=$(sh src/wc.sh "a b"); [ "$out" = "2" ]
sh test.sh >/dev/null
# CONSOLIDATION: the superseded option lists are gone from the spec. The
# decisions they produced must survive; the alternatives must not.
test -z "$(grep -F 'my recommendation' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'A `getopts` loop' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'An environment variable' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'Hand-rolled loop' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# The round-by-round Q&A block is gone too — it is a reply to a reviewer.
test -z "$(grep -F 'Q&A from planning review' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# RATIONALE SURVIVED, AND RELOCATED. The "why" was seeded ONLY inside the D1
# option block that the checklist says to strip, so this is a real question:
# it must still be in the spec, and it must now be in the Decision Log — the
# checklist's "relocate, don't delete" rule. Deleting it is the one
# unrecoverable mistake, so this is the fixture's most important assert.
grep -qiE 'getopts|parsing' specs/feature/count-flag.md
awk '/^## Decision Log/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f' specs/feature/count-flag.md | grep -qiE '(getopts|parsing|parser).*(more|longer|outweigh|than|lines)|(more|longer|outweigh|than|lines).*(getopts|parsing|parser)'
# Revision narration is stripped too — the diff and the thread carry that.
test -z "$(grep -F 'Revised after looking at the script' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'Three options were considered' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# The (a)/(b)/(c) lettering is gone OUTSIDE the Decision Log: a shipped
# Design with lettered options is an unmade decision. Scoped to exclude the
# Decision Log because relocating a rejected alternative there legitimately
# uses the same "(a) ... (b) ..." shape — see kdevkit-consolidated-resume's
# seed for the settled target-state example — so an unscoped ban would fail
# an agent for correctly relocating rather than deleting the rationale.
test -z "$(awk '/^## Decision Log/{f=1} !f' specs/feature/count-flag.md | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*-?[[:space:]]*(\*\*)?\([abc]\)' || true)"
# The requirements and the decision itself are intact — consolidation strips
# the deliberation, not the contract.
grep -qF 'R2.' specs/feature/count-flag.md
grep -qiE 'positional' specs/feature/count-flag.md
# HANDOFF GENESIS: the seeded spec has no ## Handoff block, so crossing
# planning -> dev must create one, naming the phase that is now live.
[ "$(grep -c '^## Handoff' specs/feature/count-flag.md)" = 1 ]
# It has a Phase field (not just prose), and it names dev rather than planning.
# Portable pattern: \< is a GNU awk extension and silently matches nothing
# under mawk/BusyBox, which would make this pass vacuously.
awk '/^## Handoff/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f && /^- \*\*Phase:\*\*/' specs/feature/count-flag.md | grep -q .
# The value must be one of the legal phases — and dev OR review is correct
# here, because a dev phase that reaches Push rewrites it again on the way out.
# Asserting `dev` alone would fail an agent that correctly completed the loop.
awk '/^## Handoff/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f && /Phase[^a-zA-Z]*(dev|review)([^a-zA-Z]|$)/' specs/feature/count-flag.md | grep -q .
# The block was genuinely authored, not pasted from the template placeholder.
test -z "$(grep -F 'planning | dev | review' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# Test-Gate artefact and plan ticking, as the sibling fixtures require.
grep -qF 'src/wc.sh -c' test.sh
grep -qF -e '- [x]' specs/feature/count-flag.md
test -z "$(grep -F -e '- [ ]' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# A consolidate commit exists, separate from the dev work.
git log --no-decorate --oneline base..feat/count-flag | grep -qE 'plan\(count-flag\): consolidat'
# And the spec still parses as a spec: one Requirements, one Design.
[ "$(grep -c '^## Requirements' specs/feature/count-flag.md)" = 1 ]
[ "$(grep -c '^## Design' specs/feature/count-flag.md)" = 1 ]
